{"name":"Jasper Orange","hex":"#de8f4e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jasper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any bright-coloured kind of chalcedony apart from cornelian.","An opaque, impure variety of quartz, of red, yellow, and other dull colors, breaking conchoidally with a smooth surface."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To decorate with, or as if with, jasper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jasper"},{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.\n Specifically, a sweet orange or Citrus sinensis."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jasper","blocks":[{"text":"Jasper is an opaque type of silica that comes in a variety of colors, most commonly red, yellow, brown, green, and very rarely blue. It is made mostly of very fine-grained quartz or chalcedony, with other minerals contributing to its color and appearance. The common reddish color is due to iron inclusions. Jasper breaks with a smooth surface and is used for ornamentation or as a gemstone. It can be highly polished and is used for items such as vases, jewelry, seals, and snuff boxes. The density of jasper is typically 2.5 to 2.9 g/cm3. Jaspillite is a banded-iron-formation rock that often has distinctive bands of jasper."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Jasper_outcrop_in_the_Bucegi_Mountains.jpg/330px-Jasper_outcrop_in_the_Bucegi_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jasper","wikidata":"Q201175"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Egyptian_-_Finger_Ring_with_a_Representation_of_Ptah_-_Walters_42387_-_Side_A.jpg/500px-Egyptian_-_Finger_Ring_with_a_Representation_of_Ptah_-_Walters_42387_-_Side_A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Egyptian_-_Finger_Ring_with_a_Representation_of_Ptah_-_Walters_42387_-_Side_A.jpg/500px-Egyptian_-_Finger_Ring_with_a_Representation_of_Ptah_-_Walters_42387_-_Side_A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Movable Egyptian ring in green jasper and gold, from 664 to 322 BC or later (Late Period),[5] the Walters Art Museum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEgyptian_-_Finger_Ring_with_a_Representation_of_Ptah_-_Walters_42387_-_Side_A.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Red_jasper_amulet_HARGM7392.JPG/500px-Red_jasper_amulet_HARGM7392.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Red_jasper_amulet_HARGM7392.JPG/1280px-Red_jasper_amulet_HARGM7392.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/R